A projection process change-streams events (Post 12) and updates the view:
const cs = db.collection('events').watch(
[{ $match: { operationType: 'insert' } }],
{ fullDocument: 'required' }
);
for await (const change of cs) {
const e = change.fullDocument;
if (e.aggregate !== 'order') continue;
switch (e.event_type) {
case 'order.created':
await db.collection('orders_view').insertOne({
_id: e.aggregate_id,
user_id: e.payload.user_id,
status: 'pending',
total: e.payload.total,
items: e.payload.items,
updated_at: e.occurred_at,
});
break;
case 'order.paid':
await db.collection('orders_view').updateOne(
{ _id: e.aggregate_id },
{ $set: { status: 'paid', updated_at: e.occurred_at } }
);
break;
case 'order.shipped':
await db.collection('orders_view').updateOne(
{ _id: e.aggregate_id },
{ $set: { status: 'shipped', tracking: e.payload.tracking, updated_at: e.occurred_at } }
);
break;
case 'order.cancelled':
await db.collection('orders_view').updateOne(
{ _id: e.aggregate_id },
{ $set: { status: 'cancelled', updated_at: e.occurred_at } }
);
break;
}
await persistResumeToken(change._id);
}
The read endpoint:
app.get('/orders/:id', async (req, res) => {
const o = await db.collection('orders_view').findOne({ _id: req.params.id });
if (!o) return res.status(404).end();
res.json(o);
});
Read side never touches the event log directly. Fast. Indexed. Schema designed for the read pattern.

Replaying thousands of events per command load is slow. Periodically write a snapshot of the aggregate state, then load snapshot + events_after_snapshot_version.
{
_id: "order:o123",
aggregate: "order",
aggregate_id: "o123",
version: 100,
state: { status: "shipped", items: [...], total: 99, ... },
taken_at: ISODate(...),
}
Take snapshots every N events (e.g., every 50) or on a schedule. Command handler loads snapshot + events with version > snapshot.version.
async function loadAggregate(orderId) {
const snap = await db.collection('snapshots').findOne({ _id: `order:${orderId}` });
const fromVersion = snap?.version ?? 0;
const events = await db.collection('events').find({
aggregate: 'order', aggregate_id: orderId, version: { $gt: fromVersion }
}).sort({ version: 1 }).toArray();
return events.reduce(apply, snap?.state ?? {});
}
Snapshots are derived data — disposable. Wipe + rebuild from events if your apply logic changes.

Bug in projection code? Wrote bad state? Rebuild from scratch:
// drop projection
await db.collection('orders_view').drop();
// replay all events from beginning
const cs = db.collection('events').watch([{ $match: { operationType: 'insert' } }], {
startAtOperationTime: Timestamp(0, 0) // from the start of the cluster
});
// ... feed every event through the projection logic
Or use find + sort by _id (events sorted naturally by ObjectId time) instead of change stream for a one-shot rebuild.
This retroactive correctness is one of the headline benefits of ES — no other persistence model lets you "just rerun history with the new code".

Events live forever. Their schema must evolve gracefully.
Patterns:
Add a version field per event type + handle each version in the projection.
Upcast old events to the new shape at read time.
Migration events — emit a new event ("schema migrated to v2") rather than mutating history.
Never mutate old events. The event log is sacred — modifying it breaks every projection's correctness.
